{"text": "EDUCATION\n\nMICHAEL G. GOLDSMITH\n\nTHE COLLEGE OF WILLIAM & MARY Williamsburg, VA\nMASON School of Business\nMaster of Business Administration Degree, August 2007\n\nOLD DOMINION UNIVERSITY Norfolk, VA\nMaster of Arts in Applied Sociology*, December 1998\n*Emphasis in Criminal Justice Administration\nBachelor of Arts in Sociology, December 1988\n\nEXPERIENCE\n1989-present\n\nNORFOLK POLICE DEPARTMENT Norfolk, VA\n\nPolice Captain November 2006 to present\ne Acting Assistant Chief of Police, Administrative Services Bureau (4/2011-\n7/2011). Successfully managed the Administrative Service Bureau of the Norfolk\nPolice Department during the leadership transition after Chief Bruce P. Marquis\u201d\nretirement. Started a process to provide monthly financial reporting to the Acting\nChief of Police to provide for better fiscal accountability. Started a project to\ntrack recruit demographics to increase retention and to provide for a more\nefficient recruiting process.\ne Commanding Officer, Homeland Security Division (4/2008-present).\nResponsible for leading all aspects of the Department\u2019s Homeland Security\nfunction to include the Special Operations Team, Harbor Patrol Unit, and Bomb\nSquad. Responsible for developing and implementing the Department\u2019s anti-\nterrorism strategy to include the protection of critical infrastructure and key\nresources (CIKR). Responsible for developing and updating the Department\u2019s\nEmergency Action Plan. Responsible for securing Homeland Security grant\nfunding for the Norfolk Police Department.\ne I serve as the local law enforcement representative for the Hampton Roads Urban\nAreas Working Group (UAWG). The UAWG is a multi-agency group that\nadministers the Urban Areas Security Initiative (UASI) funding for the Hampton\n\nRoads Region. In this role I am currently managing the Hampton Roads Regional.\n\nLicense Plate Reader Project for the Hampton Roads Chiefs of Police. Project\nfunding is approximately $1.2 M and will span multiple years. Currently\nmanaging the Port Security Grant Program administered through the Department\nof Homeland Security. I have successfully obtained approximately $798,000 for\nthe department\u2019s Harbor Patrol Unit. The Harbor Patrol Unit is the premier local\nlaw enforcement entity in the Norfolk Port area as a result of this funding. I have\nforged successful and beneficial relationships working with all Homeland\nSecurity entities within the Hampton Roads area.\ne Served as the lead local law enforcement planner for the Hampton Roads Full\nScale Exercise 2011. HRFSE 2011 was a multi-agency exercise that replicated a\nMumbai style attack on our area. All levels of government participated in the\nexercise as well as several local agencies. J received a letter of appreciation from\nthe Governor\u2019s Office for my work planning this exercise.\ne Commanding Officer, Office of Professional Standards (11/2006-4/2008).\nResponsible for supervising all aspects of administrative investigations\nconcerning police misconduct and citizen complaints. Responsible for advising\nthe Chief of Police on policy matters concerning the administration of discipline.",
